+# Scope and evidence
+
+This audit inspected main at `5237d1e53a2c8b34fca80f6eeb7d644a01d732e2`
+on 2026-10-16, after PRs #932, #933 and #934 merged. It used the
+software-design-philosophy diagnostics to review analyzer bindings, configuration,
+core transitions, shell/process/workspace ownership, plan/review persistence and
+remaining compatibility paths. The user permits incompatible changes. No product
+code changed, and Rust, Python product tests and Lean proofs were not rerun.
+Test references below identify acceptance coverage to run during implementation,
+not successful executions from this audit.
+
+The audit was conducted on branch `audit/2026-10-16-post934`. Existing untracked
+files in the main checkout were left untouched. Current and closed GitHub issues
+were checked to exclude already resolved findings, especially #923–#925 and
+#929–#931. Independent domain reviews were followed by parent source inspection.
+
+# Accepted improvements
+
+## One binding category per local name
+
+[Issue #935](https://github.com/HayaoSuzuki/hoimin/issues/935) tracks this change.
+
+`KnownImports` stores direct imports, module imports and type variables in three
+collections. Production insertion paths invalidate the previous name before
+changing its category. Intersections, invalidation, copying and clearing must
+therefore coordinate three collections to maintain one fact. A private
+`KnownBinding::{Direct(String), Module(String), TypeVar}` in one map removes
+these synchronization steps and makes conflicting categories unrepresentable.
+This is an internal representation change, not an observed incorrect mutation.
+[^analyzer]
+
+Keep the distinctions used by consumers: known imports exclude TypeVar;
+type variables can shadow scalar names; typing/collections.abc wildcards retain
+module bindings; class global/nonlocal projection copies the whole binding;
+replacement spelling still prefers direct aliases and uses lexical tie-breaking.
+Preserve normalized facts consumed by existing binding-flow and exception/loop
+oracles. No new Lean model is required solely to change the representation.
+Single-map spelling searches may scan additional TypeVar entries, so no speedup
+is claimed. Retain clone/scale checks and use parameterized rebinding tests and
+intersection properties to validate the change.[^analyzer]
+
+## One saved fingerprint input list
+
+[Issue #936](https://github.com/HayaoSuzuki/hoimin/issues/936) tracks this change.
+
+`PlanManifest` repeats the same fingerprint input records at the top level and
+inside `normalized_config`. Creation fills both from the same RunConfig. Header
+validation validates both lists and compares them. Verify and review use the
+top-level list, while conversion to execution configuration uses the nested list.
+Retaining only `normalized_config.fingerprint_inputs` removes the duplicated
+storage and equality check.[^plan][^config][^review]
+
+Keep validation of the retained records, current-file re-resolution, pre-execution
+and copied-workspace checks, and review store alias protection. Mutation-source
+records in `sources` have a different role and remain separate. Change the plan
+schema and reject old plans with regeneration guidance; do not introduce an old
+field adapter or dual writer. Existing plan change/deletion tests and review
+destination tests should be supplemented by malformed nested records and
+fingerprint-only protected inputs.[^plan][^review][^shell]
+
+# Rejected changes and compatibility findings
+
+| Candidate | Reason to retain the current design |
+| --- | --- |
+| All operator strings converted to enums | Canonical persisted ordering differs from enum declaration order; no new benefit beyond the already completed token-producer change was established. |
+| `all_legacy` removed based on its name | It composes the current default operator selection, not an old-format reader. |
+| Control-flow exits replaced by one enum | Several exit categories can coexist; they are not exclusive states. |
+| Lexical name-resolution and annotation flow merged | They answer different questions about deferred references and control-flow bindings. |
+| Shell process-origin bool replaced by enum | Failure accounting still needs origin metadata; renaming the discriminator does not remove ownership or routing logic. |
+| Pending-effect fields replaced by a second effect enum | Current validation compares completion identity and cleanup reservations; a second taxonomy would add synchronization. |
+| Cleanup progress merged with disk lifecycle | Attempt history and cleanup admission are distinct observations. |
+| Process start gate merged with cancellation token | Both synchronize with spawn, but the start gate blocks admission while lifecycle cancellation also terminates active work. Shared mechanics alone do not justify merging their authorities. |
+| Root cleanup booleans replaced by a phase enum | Marker publication and child-creation admission are separate facts; no redundant transition rule was established. |
+| Optional report data or generic diagnostic codes removed | Current producers and readers use these contracts; optionality alone is not a compatibility adapter. |
+| Windows delete fallback or Git fallback removed | These handle current filesystem behavior and diff shapes, not obsolete saved formats. |
+
+The decisions above follow actual consumers rather than type shape alone.
+[^analyzer][^config][^machine][^shell][^process][^workspace]
+
+Current session initialization accepts its current schema and initializes new
+databases directly; it rejects unsupported initialized versions. Earlier schema
+migrations are already gone. Historical schema fixtures are not evidence that
+production still supports those formats. No additional substantial
+compatibility-only implementation was confirmed in the inspected paths.
+[^session-schema]
+
+`RunState::with_fingerprint` has no production caller; its test helper later
+overwrites that initial fingerprint with a preflight event. This is a small
+cleanup opportunity for adjacent work, not a separate issue in this audit.
+[^machine]
+
+# Diagnostic assessment
+
+The scoped static assessment is **7.5/10**: six of eight rows have supporting
+evidence. This is not a quantitative correctness or maintainability measurement.
+
+| Diagnostic | Assessment | Evidence or action needed |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Modules have describable responsibilities | Pass in inspected domains | State transitions, analysis, execution ownership and saved-input validation have distinct responsibilities. |
+| Interfaces are simpler than implementations | Pass | Process lifecycle and checked-plan APIs hide substantial coordination and validation. |
+| Implementation knowledge can change locally | Partial; not counted | The two accepted changes remove multi-collection synchronization and duplicate saved records. |
+| Interface comments describe promises | Pass | Process waiter-drop/drain and VerifiedPlan comments document ownership and allowed changes. |
+| Reviews discuss design complexity | Pass | Recent issue plans record design checks and alternatives; this does not establish time investment. |
+| Modules own important design decisions | Pass in inspected domains | The core owns transitions; process owns child lifecycle; plan owns saved-input validation. |
+| Boundaries are understandable without full implementations | Pass at documented API level | Process and shell module contracts explain distinct shutdown responsibilities; internal complexity remains. |
+| 10–20% of effort is spent on design improvement | Unmeasured; not counted | Source and review records do not measure elapsed design effort. Measure actual effort before assigning this point. |
+
+Address the two accepted findings and measure the currently unknown investment
+row before considering 10/10. Preserve separate authorities and observations
+when simplifying; adding enum variants is not itself evidence of improvement.
+[^plan][^process][^shell][^machine][^review-process]
+
+# Self-review record
+
+Each stage received at least five distinct checks:
+
+| Stage | Checks and resulting decisions |
+| --- | --- |
+| Scope | Confirmed main SHA; confirmed #929–#931 merged; isolated audit branch; compared open/closed issues; distinguished static inspection from fresh execution. |
+| Evidence | Traced all binding insertions; traced binding consumers and wildcard rules; traced both plan-record producers; enumerated top-level consumers; checked validation and protected-input paths. |
+| Design | Tested whether categories are exclusive; retained TypeVar and alias semantics; retained distinct sources/environment data; rejected unnecessary compatibility adapters; considered single-map search costs without claiming speedup. |
+| Publication | Checked fixed-revision links; separated proposals from existing behavior; stated validation requirements; checked prior issue overlap; reviewed wording for unsupported bug/performance/proof claims. |
+
+An independent reviewer checked both issue drafts against the same source
+revision for evidence, scope, safety, acceptance tests and prior issue overlap.
+No blocking concern was found. Review's early deserialization path was added to
+the old-plan regeneration-message acceptance condition. Both issues were then
+created in `HayaoSuzuki/hoimin`.
+
+# Documentation verification
+
+The initial metadata check found a malformed `sources` line in this new audit;
+it was corrected before the final check. All 50 OKF Markdown structures then
+passed the local structure check, local links in all five changed pages existed,
+and the ten cited source files exactly matched their recorded Git revision.
+Source IDs/footnotes and root-index reachability also passed. The documentation
+builder and `git diff --check` exited successfully. Published issue bodies were
+read back and matched the reviewed drafts.
+
+Five final checks covered source-revision identity, document reachability,
+proposal-versus-implementation wording, static-versus-executed evidence, and
+publication repository/body identity. No product test or new proof result is
+implied by these documentation checks.
+
